{"records":[{"id":"9rc2n4bkmv","title":"Functional interrogation of a SARS-CoV-2 host protein interactome identifies unique and shared coronavirus host factors. Hoffmann et al.","description":"The ongoing SARS-CoV-2 pandemic has devastated the global economy and claimed more than one million lives, presenting an urgent global health crisis. To identify host factors required for infection by SARS-CoV-2 and seasonal coronaviruses, we designed a focused high-coverage CRISPR-Cas9 library targeting 332 members of a recently published SARS-CoV-2 protein interactome. We leveraged the compact nature of this library to systematically screen SARS-CoV-2 at two physiologically relevant temperatures (33 ºC and 37 ºC) along with three related coronaviruses (HCoV-229E, HCoV-NL63, and HCoV-OC43), allowing us to probe this interactome at a much higher resolution relative to genome scale studies. This approach yielded several new insights, including unexpected virus-specific differences in Rab GTPase requirements and GPI anchor biosynthesis, as well as identification of multiple pan-coronavirus factors involved in cholesterol homeostasis. This coronavirus essentiality catalog could inform ongoing drug development efforts aimed at intercepting and treating COVID-19, and help prepare for future coronavirus outbreaks.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-25T19:01:19.445Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9rc2n4bkmv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9rc2n4bkmv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"JT","last_name":"Poirier","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"r49yg49ddc","title":"Genetic screens identify host factors for SARS-CoV-2 and common cold coronaviruses. Wang et al.","description":"Supplemental Tables S1 to S5 in Microsoft Excel format for the article \"Genetic screens identify host factors for SARS-CoV-2 and common cold coronaviruses\" (Wang et al., 2020):\nTable S1: CRISPR screen results. MaGECK output for positive gene enrichment analysis of SARS-CoV-2, 229E and OC43 host factor screens. Related to Figure 1. \nTable S2: Gene ontology enrichment analysis of individual CRISPR screens and network propagation clusters. Related to Figure 2.\nTable S3: Network propagation results. Related to Figure 2. \nTable S4: RNP editing efficiencies in A549-ACE2 and Huh7.5.1-ACE2/TMPRSS2 cells. Related to Figures 3 and 5.\nTable S5: Oligonucleotide sequences used in this study. Related to STAR Methods. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-25T18:59:20.104Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r49yg49ddc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r49yg49ddc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andreas","last_name":"Puschnik","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gcdpsxd6d5","title":"RNAi screening using HK-2 cells: insights into the roles of soluble ACE2 in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Yeung et al","description":"Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) can cause acute respiratory disease and multiorgan failure. Finding human host factors that are essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection could facilitate the formulation of treatment strategies. Using a human kidney cell line—HK-2—that is highly susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, we performed a genome-wide RNAi screening and identified virus dependency factors (VDFs), which play regulatory roles in biological pathways linked to clinical manifestations of SARS-CoV-2 infection. Importantly, we discovered an overlooked role of a secretory form of SARS-CoV-2 receptor, soluble angiotensin converting enzyme 2 (sACE2), in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Further investigations revealed that SARS-CoV-2 exploits receptor-mediated endocytosis through interaction between its spike with sACE2 or sACE2-vasopressin via AT1 or AVPR1B, respectively. Our identification of new VDFs and the regulatory effect of sACE2 on SARS-CoV-2 infection shed new insights into pathogenesis and cell entry mechanism of SARS-CoV-2 as well as potential treatment strategies for COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-22T23:41:07.557Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gcdpsxd6d5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gcdpsxd6d5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Man Lung","last_name":"Yeung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","RNA Knockdown"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"72tr8jj8y4","title":"RNAi screening using HK-2 cells: insights into the roles of soluble ACE2 in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Yeung et al","description":"Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) can cause acute respiratory disease and multiorgan failure. Finding human host factors that are essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection could facilitate the formulation of treatment strategies. Using a human kidney cell line—HK-2—that is highly susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, we performed a genome-wide RNAi screening and identified virus dependency factors (VDFs), which play regulatory roles in biological pathways linked to clinical manifestations of SARS-CoV-2 infection. Importantly, we discovered an overlooked role of a secretory form of SARS-CoV-2 receptor, soluble angiotensin converting enzyme 2 (sACE2), in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Further investigations revealed that SARS-CoV-2 exploits receptor-mediated endocytosis through interaction between its spike with sACE2 or sACE2-vasopressin via AT1 or AVPR1B, respectively. Our identification of new VDFs and the regulatory effect of sACE2 on SARS-CoV-2 infection shed new insights into pathogenesis and cell entry mechanism of SARS-CoV-2 as well as potential treatment strategies for COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-22T23:48:03.555Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/72tr8jj8y4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/72tr8jj8y4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Man Lung","last_name":"Yeung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Vasopressin","Screening","RNA Knockdown","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bgxfwsg3sb","title":"A comprehensive analysis and resource to use CRISPR-Cas13 for broad-spectrum targeting of RNA viruses. Lin et al.","description":"The COVID-19 pandemic caused by SARS-CoV-2 and variants has led to significant mortality. We recently reported that an RNA-targeting CRISPR-Cas13 system, termed prophylactic antiviral CRISPR in human (PAC-MAN), offered an antiviral strategy against SARS-CoV-2 and influenza A virus. Here, we expand in silico analysis to use PAC-MAN to target a broad spectrum of human- or livestock-infectious RNA viruses with high specificity, coverage, and predicted efficiency. Our analysis reveals that a minimal set of 14 crRNAs is able to target >90% of human-infectious viruses across 10 RNA virus families. We predict that a set of 5 experimentally validated crRNAs can target new SARS-CoV-2 variant sequences with 0 mismatches. We also build an online resource (crispr-pacman.stanford.edu) to support community use of CRISPR-Cas13 for broad-spectrum RNA virus targeting. Our work provides a new bioinformatic resource for using CRISPR-Cas13 to target diverse RNA viruses in order to facilitate development of CRISPR-based antivirals. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-22T16:33:31.569Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bgxfwsg3sb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bgxfwsg3sb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xueqiu","last_name":"Lin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yanxia","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Augustine","last_name":"Chemparathy","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tara","last_name":"Pande","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marie","last_name":"La Russa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stanley","last_name":"Qi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Bioengineering","Antiviral","Computational Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"96jwcyxgn6","title":"An Airway Organoid-Based Screen Identifies a Role for the HIF1α-Glycolysis Axis in SARS-CoV-2 Infection ","description":"SARS-CoV-2 causes the COVID-19 pandemic. It is urgent to develop disease models to dissect mechanisms regulating SARS-CoV-2 infection. Here, we derive airway organoids from human pluripotent stem cells (hPSC-AOs). The hPSC-AOs, particularly ciliated-like cells, are permissive to SARS-CoV-2 infection. Using this platform, we perform a high content screen and identify GW6471, which blocks SARS-CoV-2 infection. GW6471 can also block infection of the B.1.351 SARS-CoV-2 variant. RNA-seq analysis suggests that GW6471 blocks SARS-CoV-2 infection at least in part by inhibiting HIF1α, which is further validated by chemical inhibitor and genetic perturbation targeting HIF1α. Metabolic profiling identifies decreased rates of glycolysis upon GW6471 treatment, consistent with transcriptome profiling. Finally, xanthohumol, 5-(Tetradecyloxy)-2-furoic acid, and ND-646, three compounds that suppress fatty acid biosynthesis, also block SARS-CoV-2 infection. Together, a high content screen coupled with transcriptome and metabolic profiling reveals a key role of the HIF1α-glycolysis axis in mediating SARS-CoV-2 infection of human airway epithelium.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-10-08T10:41:17.03Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/96jwcyxgn6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/96jwcyxgn6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Shuibing","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Supplemental Irrigation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7bd5bhmhmz","title":"Genome-scale identification of SARS-CoV-2 and pan-coronavirus host factor networks. Schneider et al.","description":"The COVID-19 pandemic has claimed the lives of more than one million people worldwide. The causative agent, SARS-CoV-2, is a member of the Coronaviridae family, which are viruses that cause respiratory infections of varying severity. The cellular host factors and pathways co-opted by SARS- CoV-2 and other coronaviruses in the execution of their life cycles remain ill-defined. To develop an extensive compendium of host factors required for infection by SARS-CoV-2 and three seasonal coronaviruses (HCoV-OC43, HCoV-NL63, and HCoV-229E), we performed parallel genome-scale CRISPR knockout screens. These screens uncovered multiple host factors and pathways with pan- coronavirus and virus-specific functional roles, including major dependency on glycosaminoglycan biosynthesis, SREBP signaling, BMP signaling, and glycosylphosphatidylinositol biosynthesis, as well as a requirement for several poorly characterized proteins. We identified an absolute requirement for the VTT-domain containing protein TMEM41B for infection by SARS-CoV-2 and three seasonal coronaviruses. This human Coronaviridae host factor compendium represents a rich resource to develop new therapeutic strategies for acute COVID-19 and potential future coronavirus spillover events.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-03T09:15:53.589Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7bd5bhmhmz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7bd5bhmhmz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"JT","last_name":"Poirier","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7fgg5452pr","title":"The SARS-CoV-2 interactome","description":"SARS-CoV-2 is an RNA virus whose success as a pathogen relies on its abilities to repurpose host RNA-binding proteins (RBPs) and to evade antiviral RBPs. To uncover the SARS-CoV-2 RNA interactome, we here develop a robust ribonucleoprotein (RNP) capture protocol and identify 109 host factors that directly bind to SARS-CoV-2 RNAs. Applying RNP capture on another coronavirus HCoV-OC43 revealed evolutionarily conserved interactions between coronaviral RNAs and host proteins. Transcriptome analyses and knockdown experiments delineated 17 antiviral RBPs including ZC3HAV1, TRIM25, PARP12, and SHFL and 8 proviral RBPs such as EIF3D and CSDE1 which are responsible for co-opting multiple steps of the mRNA life cycle. This also led to the identification of LARP1, a downstream target of the mTOR signaling pathway, as an antiviral host factor that interacts with the SARS-CoV-2 RNAs. Overall, this study provides a comprehensive list of RBPs regulating coronaviral replication and opens new avenues for therapeutic interventions.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-05-07T07:11:04.995Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7fgg5452pr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7fgg5452pr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sungyul","last_name":"Lee","orcid":"0000-0003-3207-1199"},{"first_name":"Young-suk","last_name":"Lee","orcid":"0000-0003-0220-9951"},{"first_name":"Yeon","last_name":"Choi","orcid":"0000-0003-4983-6573"},{"first_name":"Aheyon","last_name":"Son","orcid":"0000-0001-7945-3851"},{"first_name":"Youngran","last_name":"Park","orcid":"0000-0002-8621-1399"},{"first_name":"Kyung-Min","last_name":"Lee","orcid":"0000-0002-1373-5407"},{"first_name":"Jeesoo","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jong-Seo","last_name":"Kim","orcid":"0000-0002-8909-8440"},{"first_name":"V. Narry","last_name":"Kim","orcid":"0000-0002-3213-4965"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Proteomics","RNA","Interactome","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"jr96bw6m25","title":"Parsing the role of NSP1 in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Fisher et al","description":"SARS-CoV-2 leads to shutoff of cellular protein synthesis and expression of nsp1, a central shutoff factor in coronaviruses, inhibits cellular gene translation.\nWe reveal the multifaceted approach nsp1 uses to shut off cellular protein synthesis and uncover the central role it plays in SARS-CoV-2 pathogenesis, explicitly through blockage of the IFN response.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-05-03T14:38:47.665Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/jr96bw6m25","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/jr96bw6m25"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tal","last_name":"Fisher","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetics","Molecular Biology","Virus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":132332,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":117,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":132332,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":131775,"restricted":66,"embargoed":491},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2200},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1590},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":627},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":453},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":438},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":416},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":409},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":365},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":297},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":264}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":129716},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1901},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":182},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":133},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":74},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":65},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":55},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":41},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":30},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":1119},{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":998},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":414},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":362}]}}